# Four seam gaps from a real dispatch, as a replayable claim set.
#
#   ./scripts/seam_check.sh --claims scripts/tests/fixtures/xpr_seams.claims \
#       --settings config.settings.test --verbose > /tmp/xpr.log 2>&1; echo $?
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. A rung was once dispatched against a brief naming four
# `apps/files` seams the baseline tree did not have in the shape the brief
# claimed. A builder verified all four out-of-territory, held, and the rung
# sat ~3h serialized behind a re-dispatch. Every one was knowable from the
# baseline tree at brief time — which is the whole argument for
# `seam_check.sh`, and this fixture replays exactly those four.
#
# **The claims themselves were never committed the first time.** They were
# replayed from the brief and reported as verdicts in a report; re-verifying
# later required RECONSTRUCTING them from that report. A regression whose
# input lives in a report is a regression that gets reconstructed every time
# somebody wants it, and reconstruction is where the drift enters. So the
# input is a file now, and the replay is one command forever.
#
# THE EXPECTED VERDICTS ARE PART OF THE FIXTURE, and they differ by tree:
#
#   against the tree the dispatch was made against:
#       ALL FOUR DISPROVEN. That is the regression: the instrument catches, in
#       ~1.3s, every gap that cost that dispatch three hours.
#
#   against today's `main`:
#       THREE PROVEN, and G2 DISPROVEN **permanently and correctly** — the
#       presign-only ruling (docs/product/architecture.md, ADR files-2) means
#       `get_stream` was deliberately never built, so a brief claiming it today
#       would still be caught. G2 is not a stale claim to be fixed; it is the
#       case that proves the check still bites on a current tree.
#
# So `exit 1` is the CORRECT status here, on both trees. A run of this fixture
# that exits 0 means G2 got built after all and this comment needs rewriting;
# exit 2 means the check could not look, which outranks either.
#
# WHAT A CLAIM IS: `module.path:callable(fragment)`. The fragment lists
# parameter NAMES and nothing else — existence and signature, never behaviour,
# types, order or defaults (`scripts/seam_check.py`'s declared blind spots).

# --- G1 — a single-object delete -------------------------------------------
# The brief needed to delete one object. `apps/files` had only `delete_prefix`,
# which takes a whole tenant, so the purge declaration the brief asked for
# would have been a lie about what it could actually remove.
apps.files.storage:delete_object(tenant, key)

# --- G2 — a body read (THE PERMANENT ONE) ----------------------------------
# The brief needed to read an archive's bytes back to keep its door
# byte-stable. There was no such seam, and there is none today: the ratified
# either/or resolved it the other way — the backend signs, the client
# fetches, and the bytes never pass through us. Expected DISPROVEN forever. If
# this ever flips to PROVEN, somebody has built a body read and ADR files-2
# needs re-reading first.
apps.files.storage:get_stream(tenant, key)

# --- G3 — a bucket on the shipped template ---------------------------------
# The shipped template configured no bucket, so nothing the brief wrote could
# land anywhere on a fresh clone. This was a flagged follow-on;
# `create_bucket_if_absent` is what closed it (and `scripts/setup.sh` calls it
# per checkout since).
apps.files.storage:create_bucket_if_absent()

# --- G4 — bytes, not a stream (THE IMPORT-NOT-GREP CASE) -------------------
# The memory posture. The brief assumed a streamable body; the tree had
# `store_bytes(content: bytes)`, which would have put a whole tenant's archive
# in a worker's RAM. **A grep for the NAME goes green on the original tree and
# the rung holds anyway** — the name was right there. Only importing and
# comparing the signature catches it, which is the one argument for this
# instrument that nobody works out unprompted. `store_stream` is the seam
# that closed it.
apps.files.services:store_stream(actor, file, body)
